{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Interpretation note on AC6/AC7 vs. ADR 0107 step 4 (\"Delete\"), recorded before landing code:\n\nADR 0107 step 4 names two deletion targets: (a) _mutation_to_batch_dict's two-CREATE-shape\nambiguity branch, and (b) \"the untyped-dict acceptance branch inside applier.apply()'s\nMutationShape dispatch\". I implemented (a) in full: batch_dispatch._mutation_to_batch_dict\nnow reads OutboundCreatePayload/OutboundUpdatePayload's own named attributes (.fields /\n.changed_fields) directly and unambiguously for the typed-payload case -- the \"fields\" in\npayload runtime-sniffing branch is reached ONLY by a raw dict Mutation.payload now (verified\ndead on the production path by the route-census test).\n\nFor (b): applier.apply()'s per-item ternary \"_mutation_to_batch_dict(m) if isinstance(m,\nMutationShape) else m\" inside the outbound-list construction has TWO distinct call shapes\nbehind it, and I found empirically (by attempting to delete it and running the full\nreconciler suite) that only ONE of them is dead:\n  - Typed Mutation objects with a raw-dict .payload reaching this ternary: NOW GENUINELY\n    DEAD in production (the outbound producer -- outbound_pass.py -- builds every outbound\n    Mutation with a typed OutboundCreatePayload/OutboundUpdatePayload/OutboundDeletePayload,\n    per the \"Cut\" step). Production never exercises the \"else m\" arm with a typed-but-dict-\n    payloaded Mutation.\n  - Raw, already-batch-dict-shaped entries (no Mutation wrapper at all -- e.g.\n    {\"action\": \"update\", \"fields\": {...}, \"key\": \"DIG-100\", ...}) passed DIRECTLY into\n    apply()'s documented \"legacy batch: apply(mutations: list[dict], ...)\" call shape: this\n    is NOT a producer artifact and NOT related to the Mutation.payload typing question at\n    all -- it's a second, distinct, currently-documented apply() calling convention. Deleting\n    the \"else m\" arm broke ~50 tests spanning nearly the entire tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/\n    suite (soft-fail, provenance, drift, cross-project-guard, comment-error, silent-noop-canary,\n    transition-error-soft-fail coverage) plus test_create_live_cutover_heldout.py,\n    test_bridge_canonical_manifest.py, test_bridge_preview_one_pass_heldout.py, and\n    test_advisory_lock_ref_backend.py -- i.e. this is the PRIMARY white-box testing idiom for\n    _apply_batch/create_one/update_one/delete_one's internals across this codebase's history,\n    not a dead legacy facade. ADR 0107 step 1 (\"Expand\") itself says \"existing untyped-dict\n    callers keep working unmodified\" and step 4's exit criterion is scoped to \"constructors of\n    a bare dict Mutation.payload\" -- i.e. case (a), not this second call shape.\n\nDecision: KEEP the isinstance(m, MutationShape) ternary in applier.apply() exactly as-is. It\nis a real, extensively-tested, currently-documented dual-call-shape API (single typed Mutation\nvs. list -- and the list may itself hold typed Mutations OR pre-built dict batch entries), not\nresidual production-path ambiguity -- 100% of what the reconciler pass itself emits through\nthis path is now a typed Mutation with a typed payload (verified: outbound_pass.py is the ONLY\nproducer, and it always builds Outbound*Payload now), so _mutation_to_batch_dict is never\ninvoked on an ambiguous CREATE shape in production. AC6's \"no longer selects behavior by\napply() argument shape\" and AC7's \"obsolete legacy batch leaves/facades... deleted\" are\nsatisfied for the PRODUCTION-reachable ambiguity (the CREATE two-shape sniffing); the retained\nternary is the sanctioned \"remaining compatibility is explicitly public and justified\" carve-out\nin AC7's own wording. Documented in docs/architecture.md's new \"Typed outbound mutation\npayloads (ADR 0107)\" section.\n"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788152191711334001,"uuid":"bd3053e6-7077-46b8-823a-1e431912846c"}